  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/ultra_mode/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 289
    content: "---\nname: ultra-mode\ndescription: >-\n  Ultra mode is active for this conversation. Defines the mandatory build,\n  verify, critique and fix loop: plan acceptance checks, decouple and unit-test\n  pure logic (via node:test), make the app testable, look at the running app in\n  a real browser (via the computer-use skill), critique what you see against a\n  checklist, fix the top defects, and verify again before finishing. Read this\n  skill before your first code change in this conversation, and again only if\n  its instructions are no longer in your context.\n---\n\n# Ultra Mode: Verify What You Build\n\nUltra mode trades time for quality. The user accepts slower turns in exchange\nfor an app that has been **seen working**, not just compiled. Verification is a\nloop that runs during the build, not a final step.\n\nUse the `computer-use` skill for all browser mechanics (setup, opening pages,\nsnapshots, screenshots, videos). This skill tells you *when* to look, *what* to\njudge and *when* to stop.\n\n## 1. Before Writing Code\n\n1.  If you haven\'t already in this conversation, read the `computer-use` skill.\n    Because `playwright-cli` and headless Chromium are pre-installed in the\n    container, do not run its setup script before writing code; run the setup\n    script only if a `playwright-cli` command fails because it isn\'t installed\n    (e.g. after a container restart) or if you need to copy its extended\n    reference guides.\n2.  Write a short **verification plan** in your reasoning:\n    -   **Journeys**: the 1-3 most important things a user does in this app,\n        each as 2-5 steps with what must be *visibly true* after each step.\n        Example: \"type \'milk\' and press Enter -> \'milk\' appears at the top of\n        the list -> reload -> \'milk\' is still there\".\n    -   **Screens**: every distinct screen or state the request implies (start,\n        main, empty, loading, error, results, game over, ...).\n    -   **Reference**: if the user attached screenshots, mockups or a URL, list\n        what must match (layout, palette, lighting, typography, HUD).\n3.  Before writing code, read the matching reference file(s) in parallel:\n    -   For any **2D or 3D game**: read\n        [`references/game_title_and_hud.md`](references/game_title_and_hud.md)\n        (Title Screen, HUD, Game Shell & Gameplay Craft: controls, hitstop/juice\n        & encounter design).\n    -   For any **Three.js / WebGL or 3D scene** (including 3D games): read\n        [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md) (3D Surfaces, Scene\n        Layout, Lighting/Camera & Lossless Performance).\n4.  For follow-up requests, derive the journeys from what the user asked to\n    change, and re-check the existing journeys that the change could break.\n\n## 2. Make the App Testable\n\nBuild these in from the start. They cost little and make verification reliable:\n\n-   **Keep components modular**: Split views, forms, modals, and canvas\n    renderers into focused modules (e.g. under `src/components/` and\n    `src/utils/`, `components/` and `lib/`, or `src/app/`) rather than placing\n    the entire app in one large file. Smaller files make verification-round\n    fixes fast and surgical.\n-   **Build accessible UI. The browser sees your app through it.** Browser\n    snapshots are built from the accessibility tree. Each control shows up with\n    its role and accessible name (e.g. `button \"Add to cart\" [ref=e7]`), which\n    is how you find it and click it. Accessible UI also helps real users.\n    -   Use native elements: `<button>`, `<a href>`, `<input>` with a `<label>`,\n        `<select>`, headings, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<dialog>`. Never make a\n        clickable `<div>`. It shows up only as an unnamed `generic` node.\n    -   Every control needs a visible label or an `aria-label` (icon-only\n        buttons in particular). Make names unique and specific, e.g. \"Delete\n        \'milk\'\" rather than five buttons all called \"Delete\".\n    -   Use ARIA only where native HTML has no equivalent, e.g. tabs or\n        `aria-expanded` on a disclosure. Wrong ARIA is worse than none.\n-   Add `data-testid` only where the accessibility tree can\'t help: canvas or\n    WebGL surfaces, or containers you need to check by count or state. Snapshots\n    don\'t show `data-testid`. Use it in scripts that run against the page\n    (`getByTestId`).\n-   Ship deterministic demo or seed data, so journeys work without real\n    accounts, API keys or network responses\342\200\224but never bypass form validation by\n    silently substituting fallback values when required inputs are empty or\n    invalid.\n-   Wire actions and controls end-to-end: submitting a form, completing a flow,\n    or toggling a control must update the underlying application state (lists,\n    counts, statuses, active views) rather than only showing a static toast or\n    modal.\n-   Apps with intro or menu screens (games, onboarding, wizards) accept a\n    `?autostart=1` query parameter that skips straight to the main view.\n-   Canvas, WebGL and Three.js content is invisible to snapshots. Verify it with\n    screenshots or video, and expose the key state as attributes (e.g. `<body\n    data-state=\"playing\">` or a score element with a label).\n-   The browser here has no GPU, so WebGL / Three.js apps take the\n    software-WebGL path from the `threejs` skill (`isSoftwareGl`, on-demand\n    rendering, `afterDraw()` game loops); see\n    [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md) for budgets.\n-   For games and Three.js/WebGL scenes: follow\n    [`references/game_title_and_hud.md`](references/game_title_and_hud.md) and\n    [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md): mount the\n    `WebGLRenderer` and scene once (`useEffect(..., [])`), storing React\n    callbacks/props in `useRef` so HUD state updates never tear down the canvas\n    or reset player position; never call React `setState` at 60Hz inside\n    `requestAnimationFrame`; listen for WASD/arrow keys on `window`; and spawn\n    the player on an open path (>= 2 tiles wide) facing a clear direction, never\n    facing a wall.\n-   Games and simulations must survive the first few seconds without input.\n-   Never add debug-only UI that the user can see.\n\n## 3. Fast-Feedback Unit Tests (Logic First)\n\nBefore or alongside browser checks, verify core application logic with fast,\ndeterministic unit tests. Unit tests run in milliseconds, catch silent\nregressions, and avoid browser overhead for math, rules, and state\ntransformations.\n\n-   **Decouple logic from UI components**: Put pure calculations, game rules,\n    state machines, scoring, physics/collision math, data parsers, and win/loss\n    conditions into dedicated modules (e.g. `src/game/rules.ts`,\n    `src/utils/*.ts`, or `lib/*.ts`). Keep UI/DOM components focused purely on\n    rendering.\n-   **Write tests using Node\'s built-in test runner**: Use `node:test` and\n    `node:assert/strict` in `*.test.ts` files (e.g. `src/game/rules.test.ts` or\n    `lib/rules.test.ts`), importing modules the same way the app does\n    (extensionless relative imports):\n\n    ```ts\n    import test from \'node:test\';\n    import assert from \'node:assert/strict\';\n    import { calculateScore, checkWinCondition } from \'./rules\';\n\n    test(\'scoring logic increments correctly\', () => {\n      assert.equal(calculateScore(10, 2), 20);\n    });\n\n    test(\'win condition triggers at target threshold\', () => {\n      assert.equal(checkWinCondition({ points: 100, target: 100 }), true);\n      assert.equal(checkWinCondition({ points: 99, target: 100 }), false);\n    });\n    ```\n-   **Run tests via bash with `npx --yes tsx`**: Run each test file with\n    `npx --yes tsx --test <file>`. Plain `node --test` only strips TypeScript\n    types, so it fails on extensionless imports, type-only imports, path\n    aliases, and `.tsx`; do not use it (or `--experimental-strip-types`) for\n    TypeScript tests, and never edit app imports or types to make it work.\n    Running `npx --yes tsx --test` resolves `tsx` from `./node_modules/.bin/tsx`\n    when pre-installed (e.g., in the React starter template) without relying on\n    a global binary in `PATH`, and installs it non-interactively otherwise:\n\n    ```bash\n    npx --yes tsx --test src/game/rules.test.ts\n    ```\n-   **Hard gate**: If unit tests exist and fail, you MUST fix the logic errors\n    before proceeding to visual browser captures.\n\n## 4. Build Complete First, Then Verify\n\nImplement the complete set of requested screens, user journeys, interactive\ncontrols, form validations, and state transitions in your initial build pass\n**before** launching the first browser verification round. Do not build a thin\npartial slice and defer the rest of the app to later rounds\342\200\224once you enter\nvisual critique, it is easy to focus on surface polish and miss unbuilt\nrequirements or leave deferred handlers incomplete.\n\nScale verification effort to the change and enforce strict token budgeting:\n\n-   **Strict verification round limits**:\n    -   **Initial build (new app or feature)**: Run a maximum of 2 verification\n        rounds.\n    -   **Follow-up changes**: Run at most 1 targeted verification round.\n    -   Do not run open-ended 3+ rounds; additional rounds exponentially\n        increase token consumption and risk quota exhaustion.\n-   **Text-only or non-visual changes** (copy, configs, state/pure logic without\n    UI impact): rely on unit tests or skip browser verification entirely.\n-   **Snapshot-first evidence**: Favor `playwright-cli snapshot` over full-page\n    screenshot captures whenever verifying text, counts, form states, and DOM\n    elements. DOM snapshots consume minimal tokens compared to multimodal\n    images.\n-   **Viewport deduplication**: Do not take duplicate screenshots at both\n    desktop (1440x900) and mobile (390x844) viewports on every round. Capture\n    mobile screenshots only when explicitly evaluating responsive layouts or\n    mobile journeys.\n\n## 5. The Verification Round\n\nEach round:\n\n1.  **Run unit tests first**: Run your test files with\n    `npx --yes tsx --test <file>`. If assertions fail, fix the code before\n    touching the browser.\n2.  Make sure the app compiles cleanly (via `compile_applet`), then restart the\n    dev server (via `restart_dev_server`). Hot reload is disabled, so without a\n    restart you will look at stale code.\n3.  **First paint**: Capture at standard desktop size (1440x900). Check the\n    console for errors and failed requests.\n4.  **Mobile** (390x844): Inspect mobile viewport only if testing responsive\n    reflow, mobile navigation, or responsive UI components. Give each screenshot\n    a unique filename per round and viewport (e.g.\n    `--filename=.playwright-cli/r1-desktop.png`,\n    `--filename=.playwright-cli/r1-mobile.png`)\342\200\224never overwrite a screenshot\n    filename from an earlier capture.\n5.  **Journeys**: run each step and use `playwright-cli snapshot` for fast DOM\n    state checks (verifying that lists, counts, or validation errors updated),\n    taking a screenshot only on the key visual states.\n6.  **Motion**: for animations, transitions, scrolling, canvas, 3D or physics,\n    record a short video instead of a still.\n7.  **Inspect every capture.** Foreground captures under `.playwright-cli/` are\n    attached or inlined directly in the command or tool response; review them\n    before moving on (read `/.playwright-cli/<name>` manually only if a command\n    finished in the background).\n\n## 6. Critique Before You Fix\n\nAfter each round, judge the captures as a strict senior product designer would,\nnot as their author. Write down the **top 3-5 defects**, most severe first, each\nwith a concrete fix, **before** editing any code.\n\n-   **Request fidelity**: every requested feature and screen exists and works;\n    nothing unrequested clutters the UI.\n-   **Journeys**: each step\'s expected visible state actually appears; no dead\n    clicks or console errors.\n-   **Visual hierarchy**: one clear primary action per screen; obvious reading\n    order.\n-   **Layout and spacing**: consistent alignment and spacing; nothing overlaps,\n    clips, overflows or floats loose.\n-   **Typography**: deliberate type scale; readable sizes; no default or\n    mismatched fonts.\n-   **Color and contrast**: coherent palette; readable contrast; no generic\n    neon-on-dark or glow everywhere.\n-   **Responsiveness**: the mobile frame is usable: no horizontal scroll,\n    tappable targets, nothing cut off.\n-   **States and polish**: empty, loading and error states exist; hover and\n    focus states; smooth transitions.\n-   **Reference match**: if the user gave a reference, layout, palette, lighting\n    and style match it side by side.\n\nSeverity guide:\n\n-   **High**: a broken journey, an uncaught exception, a failed API request or\n    broken visible asset, a console message that points to a real bug (broken\n    component state, invalid DOM nesting, invalid rendering math), unreadable\n    text, overlapping or clipped UI, a missing requested feature, or an obvious\n    mismatch with the user\'s reference.\n-   **Medium**: weak hierarchy, inconsistent spacing or type, poor mobile\n    layout, missing states.\n-   **Low**: minor polish.\n-   **Ignore**: console messages that have no user-visible impact\342\200\224a missing\n    `favicon.ico`, library deprecation notices where the fallback works, browser\n    or driver performance chatter, dev-tool banners, and expected fallback logs\n    when optional services aren\'t configured. Never spend an edit and rebuild\n    round just to silence harmless console output.\n\n## 7. Fix, Then Verify Again\n\n-   Fix the defects in severity order using targeted partial edits to the\n    affected functions, handlers, or JSX sections rather than overwriting whole\n    files from scratch (and never rewrite multiple large files in a single\n    turn), and never remove existing form validation or state handlers when\n    adjusting layout or styling. Then run another round.\n-   A check is only valid if it ran **after your last edit**. Clean compilation\n    (`compile_applet`), unit tests (`npx --yes tsx --test`), and browser\n    captures must pass after your last code modification. Never finish on a\n    stale check, a broken build, or failing unit tests.\n-   Stop when no high-severity defects remain and the journeys pass, or after\n    reaching the round limit (max 2 for initial builds, 1 for follow-ups). If\n    defects remain, fix the ones you can and report the rest.\n\n## 8. When the Browser Misbehaves\n\n-   Take a snapshot before clicking, and target elements by their snapshot\n    reference. Text matches often hit several elements.\n-   If a control you expect is missing from the snapshot, or has no name, treat\n    it as an accessibility bug in the app and fix it. Don\'t work around it with\n    coordinates.\n-   Only controls that are visible and clickable get a reference. If a control\n    has no reference, something (a modal or overlay) is probably covering it:\n    interact with that first. **Never change the app\'s UI just to make an\n    automated click work.**\n-   If a command hangs or times out, kill the browser and fall back to cheaper\n    evidence: a DOM snapshot, console output, or a page with `?autostart=1`.\n    Don\'t retry the same command more than once.\n-   If clicking freezes the page, treat it as a real performance bug, because\n    users will feel it too. Fix it, then verify again.\n-   Close the browser when a round is done.\n\n## 9. Report Honestly\n\nEnd the turn with a brief summary for the user:\n\n-   What you verified (unit tests, journeys, viewports) and what you fixed\n    because of it.\n-   Anything you could not verify, and why.\n\nNever claim you saw or tested something that you didn\'t.\n"
    num_lines: 290
